00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.040
And this is this
article that's titled,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.720
"Is There Life on Other Planets?

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.810
Google Cloud Is Working with
NASA's Frontier Development Lab

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.620
to Find Out."

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:48.820
So it's really
interesting, actually.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.820
It talks a little bit
about these two approaches

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.450
that they've broken down into
researching the biologies

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.970
of distant planets.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.890
And one approach is,
in particular, they're

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.690
modeling the possible
atmospheres that

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.040
could exist on distant planets.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.670
And they analyzed more than
270,000 simulated atmospheres

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:04.844
on Compute Engine.

00:02:04.844 --> 00:02:06.760
And this modeling that
they did, they actually

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.590
have code that they've
shared on GitHub.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.742
It's called PyAtmos.

00:02:09.742 --> 00:02:12.200
And there's a link in the blog
post that you can check out.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.380
And then the second approach
is that they're then

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.270
generating an
actual spectral data

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.954
set of over 3 million rocky
terrestrial exoplanets

00:02:19.954 --> 00:02:20.870
with machine learning.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.050
So apparently we don't
have enough data yet,

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.044
but we have a ton
of visual images

00:02:26.044 --> 00:02:28.210
that have been captured by
different satellites that

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.460
exist, and then they've
done a significant amount

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.180
of simulating as well, to try
to help come up with good models

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.200
to identify rocky terrains that
would potentially have life.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.940
So that data set,
they're planning

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.980
to release sometime
in the near future,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.000
and you can keep
an eye out for it.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.020
But you should
check this blog out.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:44.980
It's interesting.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.480
And just hearing about what's
going on in that space,

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.350
and how machine learning
technology is playing a role.

