the community in Nairobi.

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.350
So then we had a
chat with Shakir,

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:24.780
and the rest of the team.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:26.500
We seemed to like
each other, and we

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.402
started the process of
planning the Indaba this year.

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:31.110
MELANIE WARRICK: And
the rest is history.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:32.900
Now you're in, and you're
never going to leave.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.530
KARIM BEGUIR: Yes, and we'll be
hosting 1,000 people next year,

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:36.655
so that should be exciting.

00:09:36.655 --> 00:09:38.488
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh,
that's really exciting.

00:09:38.488 --> 00:09:40.930
Karim, you're here this year
because you're speaking.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.070
Can you tell us a
little bit about what

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:42.880
you're talking about?

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:43.671
KARIM BEGUIR: Yeah.

00:09:43.671 --> 00:09:46.150
So my session's going to be
on Thursday about the life

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:48.310
of a machine learning startup.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:51.580
And the goal is really
to give some insights

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:55.090
about what it takes to build
a startup, but specifically,

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:58.360
a machine learning startup,
and specifically, in Africa.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.910
So looking forward to
share some knowledge,

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.520
and also raise the
awareness in the AI

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.410
community about
the opportunities

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.834
that there are for AI in Africa.

00:10:06.834 --> 00:10:09.000
MELANIE WARRICK: What are
some of the opportunities?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.020
KARIM BEGUIR: I think we
have tremendous talent.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.904
And one of the insights
that I want to convey

00:10:15.904 --> 00:10:17.570
is that, in today's
world, it's actually

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:20.720
possible to build a
high-quality machine learning

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.400
startup in Africa.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.950
All the sources of
knowledge are there.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.290
The sources of
high-quality mentoring

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:30.440
are there now, with events
such as Deep Learning Indaba.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.400
So really make
the new generation

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.000
aware of what's possible, and
share some of the enthusiasm

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.560
and some of the
experience that we've

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:40.470
been through at InstaDeep.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.178
MELANIE WARRICK: And
tell us a little bit

00:10:42.178 --> 00:10:45.470
about what InstaDeep
does, how it started.

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.160
KARIM BEGUIR: So InstaDeep
is an AI startup.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.900
It's an African AI
startup which bridges

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.950
the gap between advanced AI
research and large companies

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:57.480
that have real business needs.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.390
So the way we've done that is
by developing our own research,

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:06.470
but being able to explain and
convey to the communities,

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:12.320
to the companies, how advanced
AI can benefit to them.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.800
So that has been our experience.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.960
And what we've discovered
by doing that-- also,

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.660
the project started in
North Africa, in Tunisia.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.270
Started as a
bootstrap, literally.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.130
My co-founder Zora and I
started with two laptops

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.960
and a lot of enthusiasm.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.870
And what we've discovered
is that it's actually

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.100
possible to bring
promising African talent

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:36.140
to competitive levels in AI.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:40.310
To be able to add real values to
companies in Africa and beyond.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:43.520
That's the main takeaway, and
that's also my main message.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.520
We have to be optimistic
about AI in Africa.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.790
The opportunity is tremendous.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.250
And I think this is our agenda.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.150
All here to convey
that message, and make

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:54.150
sure it's widely spread.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.775
MELANIE WARRICK: It's definitely
not a hidden agenda, for sure.

00:11:56.775 --> 00:11:59.210
Well, in terms of InstaDeep,
when we were talking offline,

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:01.175
you were telling me that it's
in five different locations.

00:12:01.175 --> 00:12:02.160
KARIM BEGUIR: Exactly.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:03.190
MELANIE WARRICK: And
where are those locations?

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.750
KARIM BEGUIR: So we
started initially in Tunis.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.270
In Africa, now we
have three locations.

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:09.560
We're operational in Kenya.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.930
That's the team
that Muthoni leads.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.440
We are also operational
in Lagos, Nigeria.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.000
And we also have offices
in Paris and London.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.200
So five locations,
and looking forward to

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.314
continue to expand our
presence in Africa.

00:12:23.314 --> 00:12:24.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:25.813
And Muthoni, how did
you get involved?

00:12:25.813 --> 00:12:27.780
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: Through
my community work.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.880
Karim had visited Nairobi
because he's a Google Launchpad

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:33.020
Accelerator mentor.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.570
So Nairobi is quite
the city, and you just

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:36.920
fall in love immediately.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:41.030
So he was interested in
setting up in Nairobi,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:44.190
and was meeting with people
within the community.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.240
So that's how I met
with Karim, and was

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:51.350
sold on his vision of
growing AI in Africa,

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:54.470
and providing opportunities
for a billion people

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.750
to work on exciting projects.

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:58.250
And yeah, the rest
is history again.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:12:59.791
MELANIE WARRICK:
The rest is history,

00:12:59.791 --> 00:13:01.520
and now you're working
with the group.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.250
In terms of-- you'd mentioned
about the Google Launchpad

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.270
Accelerator.

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:08.406
I know you're also a GDE,
a Google Developer Expert.

00:13:08.406 --> 00:13:09.780
I also know we
talked about this,

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.180
that you're using Google
Cloud products as well.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.430
What are some of the products
that you're currently

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:14.560
using at InstaDeep?

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:18.080
KARIM BEGUIR: TensorFlow
is our main platform in AI.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.130
It's a tremendously
powerful tool.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.910
I think people do not realize
how powerful this tool is.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:26.390
And we're really grateful
to have that free access

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.200
that Google has provided.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:31.460
So in my experience
as also an MLGD,

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:34.460
I've come to appreciate all
the work that Google is doing.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:37.790
Particularly, for example,
products such as the Assistant

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.900
are tremendously powerful,
and make advanced AI

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.481
available to all start-ups.

00:13:43.481 --> 00:13:46.105
I mentioned another one, which
I think is extremely impressive.

00:13:46.105 --> 00:13:49.180
It is Auto Machine
Learning, AutoML.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:51.320
That is a
groundbreaking product.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.500
It effectively enables
start-ups all over the world--

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.790
and including in Africa-- to
have access to high-quality AI

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:00.980
without needing extended teams.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:05.540
So participating too,
Launchpad Accelerator Africa

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.940
with Google, helping
build the ecosystem

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.860
has been also a
tremendous opportunity

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.230
for me to meet great people.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.220
And it's through
those channels that I

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.100
have come to meet with Muthoni.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:18.440
And also being
convinced that now

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.420
is the time to make a difference
on the ground in Africa.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.140
So it has encouraged me
and acted as a catalyst

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:25.910
to the vision I initially had.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:26.826
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:14:26.826 --> 00:14:29.004
And you were mentioning
about communities, Muthoni.

00:14:29.004 --> 00:14:30.920
I do want to talk a
little bit about-- there's

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:32.545
a community in
particular that you run,

00:14:32.545 --> 00:14:35.255
which is the Nairobi
Women in Machine Learning.

00:14:35.255 --> 00:14:37.130
How did you get involved
with that community?

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:41.330
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: The community
with Kathleen two years ago.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.180
I had just graduated
from my undergrad

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.930
and was working in government.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.900
The Kenya Open Data Initiative.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:51.860
And there was a lot of buzzwords
around big data, AI, and all

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:52.430
that.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.380
And there was a
lot of expectations

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.630
in our scope of work.

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:59.250
So we were learning
a lot of new skills,

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:03.380
but I felt the need to connect
with people who were working

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.530
in a similar space so we can
share ideas, or resources,

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:10.020
or just know what is happening
around the world together.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.230
So that's how I
learned about the Women

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:14.960
in Machine Learning and
Data Science community,

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.540
and reached out
to them, and they

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.260
were happy to help us
get set up in Nairobi.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:21.990
And then after
that, we did a call

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:25.260
for volunteers who are
interested in helping

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:26.790
build the community.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.540
So two years later, we
run a lot of workshops,

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:36.420
from introductory classes on
how to use Python and R to more

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:41.850
advanced classes on TensorFlow
and other advanced AI concepts.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.040
And it's very
interesting, the range

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.110
of people attending the lessons.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:48.780
For example, we have
university lecturers

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:52.620
coming to learn Python,
or 13-year-old girl

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.460
coming to learn how to program.

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:56.940
So it's been a very
interesting experience,

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.595
building that
community in Nairobi.

00:15:58.595 --> 00:16:00.844
MELANIE WARRICK: How large
is the community right now?

00:16:00.844 --> 00:16:04.260
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: We have close
to 1,800 people on meet-up.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:08.340
We probably have met
700 of the people.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:09.570
So it's a huge group.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:13.920
And how we run things is
that it's very volunteer-led.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:16.080
So a lot of people
will come to us

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.450
and then see a need
in the community,

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:21.940
and we let them champion that as
a project within the community.

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.190
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:24.148
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: Yeah.

00:16:24.148 --> 00:16:25.920
One interesting one
that I would mention

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:30.360
is, we did a master class
celebrating the International

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:31.590
Women's in March.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:35.190
We did a master class
introduction in Python and R.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:38.260
And the ladies attending were
very, very interested in it.

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:41.460
And two of them volunteered
to grow that class,

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.910
and now they are doing a
course across four months,

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:49.410
and have a class of about 30
ladies in Nairobi and in Kenya

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:52.630
doing their course by
themselves remotely,

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:55.110
and then giving feedback
every week, and results,

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:56.485
and sharing
knowledge and skills.

00:16:56.485 --> 00:16:57.901
MELANIE WARRICK:
What did they use

00:16:57.901 --> 00:16:59.436
to help work remotely together?

00:16:59.436 --> 00:17:00.810
MUTHONI WANYOIKE:
They use Slack.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.850
So they coordinate
class and Slack.

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:08.310
We took the "R for Data Science"
workbook by Hadley Wickham,

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:11.790
and then divided that across,
and made that a curriculum

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.430
spread out over four months.

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:17.280
And then we use Slack to
coordinate work, as well

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:19.079
as GitHub, because
a lot of the people

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.569
also were not using
GitHub, and that

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:24.630
really counts when you're, for
example, applying for a job.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.640
So now, they can upload
their projects there,

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:30.030
and they're able to showcase
their work when dealing

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:31.410
with recruiters, for example.

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:32.410
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:34.020
I'm pretty impressed
at how important

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:35.580
GitHub has become in the space.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:37.164
And when I talk to
people, and I tell

00:17:37.164 --> 00:17:38.580
them all the time--
and even here,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.496
I was talking to people
about how important it

00:17:40.496 --> 00:17:41.850
is to publish in GitHub.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.270
To not wait for
it to be perfect.

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:45.600
To just get it out there,
and show that you're

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.100
working on something.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.220
Well, so do you collaborate with
other Women in Machine Learning

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.440
communities, or other
communities in Nairobi

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:53.640
or outside of Nairobi?

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:54.870
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: We do.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.915
So for example, just out
of us having the community

00:17:57.915 --> 00:18:00.090
in Nairobi, now we
have a community

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:03.020
in Lagos of women
in machine learning.

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:07.680
We also have worked with the few
women taking the data science

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.120
class in Uganda.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:12.840
And just at the Indaba,
I've met very many ladies

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:16.020
who are very excited to
have similar communities

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:17.020
in their countries.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:21.840
So very, very exciting times
for building the skills of women

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.760
in Africa for machine learning.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:24.826
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:18:24.826 --> 00:18:26.450
Sounded like you said
it was a meet-up,

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:29.820
so if people want to explore
or check out the group,

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:31.466
that's where they can find it.

00:18:31.466 --> 00:18:33.840
Karim, what is community-- we
talked a little bit already

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.590
about this, but what
does that mean to you

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:38.190
in terms of these types of
communities, and your company,

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:40.055
and the interest
that you have in AI?

00:18:40.055 --> 00:18:42.180
KARIM BEGUIR: So I think
it's very important

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:45.820
to help communities
understand the AI opportunity.

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:48.810
So a lot of what
we do is actually

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:52.170
trying to bring that
know-how, and helping people

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:53.670
focus on what really matters.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:56.610
I would say, today, the problem
is not the lack of information.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:59.400
This may be like an
information overflow.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:02.130
So a big part of what
I do is I mentor.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:05.740
And we also organize TensorFlow
meet-ups, very active.

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:07.260
We organize hackathon.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.780
A month ago, I was in Lagos
organizing a hackathon.

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:11.490
I was really impressed
by the talent

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:14.880
and the passion of the young
students and engineer there.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:18.450
So a big part of what we
do is raise awareness.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:21.390
Break a sort of
mental idea that, oh,

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.850
you cannot be
world-class in Africa.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.210
As an African AI startup, you
can compete with the best.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:30.450
And we had really very
constructive exchanges

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:34.020
here at Deep Learning
Indaba with Nando Freitas,

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.150
and David Silver from DeepMind.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:39.180
And their feedback was
really very positive,

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.090
and this encouraged us
to continue to share

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:43.980
the message all through Africa.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:46.740
That AI is a real opportunity.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:49.185
That you can be
world-class in AI.

00:19:49.185 --> 00:19:53.659
All what it takes is positivity,
willingness to learn, energy,

00:19:53.659 --> 00:19:55.950
and I believe, in the continent,
we have a lot of that.

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.260
MELANIE WARRICK: And any
advice or recommendations

00:19:58.260 --> 00:19:59.940
for those companies
that are considering

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:03.780
looking at having their own
offices in parts of Africa?

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.870
KARIM BEGUIR: I think
the key thing in AI--

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:07.410
and in many other things--

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:09.760
is focusing on the people.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:12.150
So, for example,
at InstaDeep, we

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.490
do not have a
specific plan that we

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:16.930
should be, for example,
having an office

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.450
in a particular country or city.

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:21.730
It is really driven by
the quality of the people.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:25.360
When we feel we have
the right partners--

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.190
and we're so excited to
have Muthoni in Kenya.

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:32.000
We have a great machine
learning team also in Nigeria.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.630
So when you have the right
team, the right people,

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:36.820
you can build.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:38.050
And that's my main message.

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:39.675
And this is, I think,
it's particularly

00:20:39.675 --> 00:20:41.050
relevant in Africa.

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:44.350
It's about investing
on the right people who

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.490
share your vision, who
share your enthusiasm.

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:49.210
So my message to companies
looking, potentially,

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:53.650
at hiring African talent
is, try to look actively

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:55.044
for the top talent.

00:20:55.044 --> 00:20:56.710
And most importantly,
for the people who

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:57.876
have a willingness to learn.

00:20:57.876 --> 00:20:59.740
A willingness to
challenge themselves

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:03.010
to get to a certain
level of competitiveness

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.160
and collaboration
within the team.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:08.590
So I've been really surprised
by how quickly we've

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:10.270
met some of the right people.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:13.360
And at the end of the day,
in AI or anything else,

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:14.210
it's people-driven.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:15.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Very much so.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:16.060
I agree.

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:18.430
And appreciate that
too, from the standpoint

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:21.010
of talking about the
community-building,

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:24.340
talking about the corporation,
having that opportunity

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:27.720
to explore these communities
and see where the talent is.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:29.470
And then also giving
back, and giving back

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:31.610
in terms of the
training is phenomenal.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:33.610
I know we talked about
this earlier already.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.068
You were touching on some of
the tools that are being used.

00:21:36.068 --> 00:21:38.022
When you bring in
your talent, it

00:21:38.022 --> 00:21:40.480
sounds like you're doing some
of your own training as well,

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:41.620
and helping people grow.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:42.315
Is that correct?

00:21:42.315 --> 00:21:43.285
KARIM BEGUIR: Yes, absolutely.

00:21:43.285 --> 00:21:43.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:45.550
And so what are some
of the key skills,

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:47.700
in particular, that you're
trying to build out?

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.750
KARIM BEGUIR: So we believe that
it's possible to take motivated

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:54.880
and young student or engineer--
let's say undergrads,

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:57.250
or completing their
grad studies--

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:59.950
and get them very
quickly on to working

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.830
on AI research
problems which would

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:05.320
be considered like PhD-plus.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.260
I think that's been one of
the fascinating surprises I've

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.990
had building the
company, is to see

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:12.650
that this is actually possible.

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:14.320
We had great success stories.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:18.570
Some fantastic women,
students we had on the team.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.550
InstaDeep really
believes in diversity,

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:24.496
and making sure everybody
has an equal opportunity.

00:22:24.496 --> 00:22:26.620
And we've been really
surprised by success stories.

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:30.430
We've had interns push the
state of the art of work

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.170
that's been done
in Silicon Valley.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:34.820
So this is, I believe,
an exciting time.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:36.790
And the key message
I want to convey

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:39.610
is, do not believe
the AI opportunity is

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:41.440
something remote from you.

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:44.620
If you're truly motivated,
if you truly want to learn,

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:46.990
there is a way, and
there are mentors

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:50.380
available to help, whether me or
all the amazing people at Deep

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:53.867
Learning Indaba, to make that
opportunity a reality for you.

00:22:53.867 --> 00:22:56.200
MELANIE WARRICK: What are
some of the resources that you

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:59.854
find more valuable for teaching,
and for helping others train?

00:22:59.854 --> 00:23:01.770
Because I know we talked
about Hadley Wickham,

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:04.210
and how his book has helped
with some of the training

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.090
that you're working on
with the Nairobi Women

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:06.820
in Machine Learning.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:08.650
Karim, do you have any
ones that particularly

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.210
come to mind that you
find very valuable?

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:11.590
KARIM BEGUIR: Yeah, absolutely.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:13.210
There are a couple
of books which

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:16.150
we found to be tremendous
resources in practice.

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:18.090
The good news is that
they're for free.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.327
All what it takes is
go and click a link

00:23:20.327 --> 00:23:21.160
and you'll get them.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:24.310
Download the PDF, print it,
and here you're in business.

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:26.830
So I say, one major
book if you're

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:30.340
interested in deep learning
is the "Deep Learning"

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:33.660
book by Ian Goodfellow,
Courville, and Bengio.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:34.930
That is a fantastic one.

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.580
And I love to say
it's a fantastic

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:38.170
one, because it's a real test.

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:41.590
It starts really easy,
and the difficulty almost

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:43.790
increases exponentially
chapter by chapter.

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:46.630
So if you're motivated, take
the Goodfellow, and make sure

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:47.590
you finish it.

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:50.875
You'll be a long way into a
positive and successive career

00:23:50.875 --> 00:23:52.212
in AI.

00:23:52.212 --> 00:23:54.670
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: I'd like to
mention something interesting

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:58.930
that motivates me at InstaDeep
in terms of growing talent.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:02.350
So we also have very
experienced researchers

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:05.070
working at InstaDeep as
well, and they somehow

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:08.770
are paired with our
junior engineers.

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:11.590
And they're able to
work together very well.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:13.750
So that really
helps our engineers

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:17.795
grow at a really, really fast
rate in terms of their skill.

00:24:17.795 --> 00:24:18.295
Yeah.

00:24:18.295 --> 00:24:20.044
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
a common challenge

00:24:20.044 --> 00:24:22.870
too with most companies, is
having the ability to bring

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:25.090
in junior talent, and
having them be able to grow,

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:27.490
and the fact that you're
actually mentoring and setting

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:28.821
up that kind of relationship.

00:24:28.821 --> 00:24:30.070
Some companies are doing that.

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:32.616
Not everybody, unfortunately,
is able to execute on it.

00:24:32.616 --> 00:24:33.490
They're not doing it.

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:34.780
They're not executing on it.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:36.730
KARIM BEGUIR: And I
think Muthoni's touching

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:38.090
an important point.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:41.110
At InstaDeep, we make
sure that all projects

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:42.700
are cross-regional.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:46.030
So different teams
have to collaborate.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:48.850
And we've experienced
this in particular

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:51.550
with the teams we
have also in Europe.

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:53.950
We find it to be
a key component.

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:57.260
Everybody gets to the
same access to resources.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.470
Everybody gets to
share and collaborate.

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:02.410
And we've had tremendously
interesting experiences

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:03.100
with this.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:06.490
African talent can pick
it up really quick.

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:09.760
And we've had cases where,
really, the innovation is

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.990
driven from Africa, and
we're very proud of that.

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:12.990
MELANIE WARRICK: How so?

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:16.050
KARIM BEGUIR: So we've had,
for example, research projects.

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:18.030
I'll mention a
recent article which

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:21.380
published called "Rank
Rewards," which is effectively

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:24.330
taking the work that had
been pioneered by DeepMind,

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:28.410
and David Silver in particular,
around two-player games.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:29.920
This is very interesting.

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:31.500
It's a massive breakthrough.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.500
Actually, technically, AI
was invented by Alan Turing

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:35.910
to solve chess.

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:39.780
So you could say AlphaZero is
really like a moment in time.

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:42.240
And we were very excited
by those breakthroughs.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:46.800
And we tried to think about, how
could this breakthrough apply

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:51.010
to real use cases, real
businesses, real situations,

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:54.730
whether it's around
transportation or logistics?

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:56.430
How to make those useful?

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.160
This has led us to
breakthroughs which

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:03.150
were pioneered in particular by
our North African team, which

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:06.180
got us to great
reviews, great feedback,

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:09.360
and ultimately, building
next-generation products

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:13.100
that will benefit our team,
our communities, and beyond.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.350
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:16.224
So off of that point
about working remotely--

00:26:16.224 --> 00:26:18.630
and you had this great example
in terms of what was being

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.020
driven from Africa--

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:21.645
what are some of the
biggest challenges

00:26:21.645 --> 00:26:24.590
that you've had working with
remote teams working together?

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:27.600
KARIM BEGUIR: I think it's
important to make sure people

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.970
communicate and collaborate.

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:33.720
So some of the challenges
are more practical.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.540
For example, when we
organize an off-site,

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:37.800
make sure you're going
to get the visas,

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:42.060
because unfortunately, it's very
hard to travel around Africa.

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:45.610
So problems are
practical in nature.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:49.800
But if you are really motivated,
you could go beyond them

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.310
and create a culture
of collaboration.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.240
So at InstaDeep, we
really believe in that.

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:58.200
We make sure, at least twice
a year, the whole global team

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:03.150
meets in a location to exchange
and share directly, and build

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.430
up an inclusive culture.

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:10.710
So software is, I believe, the
way to go in Africa due to some

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:14.010
of the difficulties around
moving people, moving goods.

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:15.950
So we still struggle
sometimes with visas.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:17.460
And Muthoni knows about this.

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:21.360
We have to fight to get her
in at our list of sites.

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:24.390
But in startup, like
in everything else,

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:25.860
it's about motivation.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.480
If you really have
the motivation

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:31.680
to go beyond difficulties,
ultimately, in today's world,

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:32.490
you can.

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:35.760
And the point I want to also
make is that you're not alone.

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.200
If you're an African
machine learning startup,

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:42.000
you have the chance to
access to tech giants who

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.920
are there to help you to
develop the ecosystem.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:47.610
And I found that to be
really transformational.

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.590
This also has guided
me towards the work

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:53.474
I do with Google as a
machine learning dev expert.

00:27:53.474 --> 00:27:54.890
There are opportunities
out there,

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:57.000
so don't believe that
you're on your own.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:57.570
You're not.

00:27:57.570 --> 00:27:59.220
There are lots of
people who are super

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:01.860
excited about the
potential of the continent,

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:03.990
and will actually lend
a hand and help you.

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.190
That's been our experience.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.110
And if we can do
the same and share

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.490
that feedback and that
positive feedback,

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:11.087
I think we've gone a long way.

00:28:11.087 --> 00:28:12.920
MELANIE WARRICK: I see
you nodding, Muthoni.

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:14.649
Anything else you wanted to add?

00:28:14.649 --> 00:28:17.190
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: Now just to
agree that it's quite difficult

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:18.900
to move around the continent.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:23.040
But it's interesting to see
the effort towards just making

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.710
things happen, because,
for me, representation

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:26.790
is very important.

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:30.240
And sometimes, just this
limit on moving-- for example,

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.810
we had colleagues who
were to attend the Indaba

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.460
and couldn't get their visas.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:39.210
That means it blocks a
part of them accessing

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:40.920
resources and people
who would have

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:44.250
been key to their
next phase of life.

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:47.130
But with technology,
we are able to bridge

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:51.482
some of these problems, and it
becomes an easier way of life.

00:28:51.482 --> 00:28:53.940
MELANIE WARRICK: I love the
fact that you're talking about,

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:55.860
particularly, how
all of your teams

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:57.495
have the same resource access.

00:28:57.495 --> 00:29:00.490
And that that's what
you fight to achieve.

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:02.395
But I also very
much value the fact

00:29:02.395 --> 00:29:04.770
that you're trying to make
sure that all these teams work

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:06.210
together.

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:08.170
I've worked with
remote teams as well,

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:09.570
and I feel like
I'm stronger when

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:11.620
I work with people who are
from different backgrounds,

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.570
different mindsets.

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:14.790
And it sounds like that's
a large part of what's

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:17.280
driving this, and also,
what you experience as well.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:17.850
Correct?

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:20.790
KARIM BEGUIR: You have to create
a culture of inclusiveness

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:22.470
and empowerment.

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:25.860
All our teams are treated the
same no matter where they are.

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.450
We value ideas.

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.240
We value intellectual curiosity.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:32.820
So we've had cases
where somebody

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:35.130
was supposed to
work on something

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:37.470
but turned out to be
brilliant at something else.

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:40.330
We love that kind of
positive surprises.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.260
And if you create an
environment which is positive,

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:46.320
which pushes people
up and rewards

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:49.650
constant learning,
intellectual curiosity,

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:51.495
you are bound to
have good surprises.

00:29:51.495 --> 00:29:54.270
MUTHONI WANYOIKE: People's
motivation to do things cannot

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:55.980
be overstressed.

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:58.920
When people have their
own personal motivation

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:03.120
to work on projects, then
it just makes, for example,

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:06.510
even the small
challenges of maybe time,

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:09.030
because you're working
across different time zones.

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:10.950
But when you're
motivated to work

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:14.490
towards exciting challenges,
it makes some of those things--

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:17.390
you easily overlook
them, I think.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:17.890
Yeah.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:19.160
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:21.610
Well, anything else
that we didn't already

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:23.260
cover that you
wanted to touch on,

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:26.020
that you wanted to emphasize,
whether it's about InstaDeep,

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:29.760
about building communities,
about Deep Learning Indaba?

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:31.720
KARIM BEGUIR: Well,
to conclude, I

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:34.210
think it's really an
exciting time for Africa,

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:37.810
because there is a
transformative technology, AI,

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:40.450
which is happening, and
will continue to happen

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:42.310
for the foreseeable future.

00:30:42.310 --> 00:30:44.440
And at the same
time, the resources

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:48.130
are progressively there to make
taking opportunities, building

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:51.680
your businesses, employing
people a reality.

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:54.610
So we're super excited
about this at InstaDeep,

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:57.010
and within the Deep
Learning Indaba.

00:30:57.010 --> 00:31:00.250
It's a community of
like-minded people

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:02.300
who really sense
the opportunity.

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:07.000
So I think this is a historic
opportunity for Africa.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.590
And we're super
excited about it.

00:31:09.590 --> 00:31:13.760
And ultimately, it's about
building all this together.

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:16.540
This is the message of
Deep Learning Indaba.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:17.770
We build together.

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:20.180
And that will make a difference.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:23.050
So my message to
anybody who's listening

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:26.920
is, do not believe that
this is beyond you.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:28.510
Be part of it.

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:32.740
Bottoms-up approach,
ultimately, is the way to go.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:35.100
This is what will change Africa.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.110
And all what it takes
is a bit of motivation,

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:42.079
a bit of curiosity, and not
being afraid to get started.

00:31:42.079 --> 00:31:43.120
Great things will happen.

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:44.350
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's wonderful.

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:45.700
And that actually
reminds me of something

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:47.033
I heard you say the other night.

00:31:47.033 --> 00:31:48.460
Muthoni was saying yes.

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:51.790
Saying yes to things that also
things that feel like they're

00:31:51.790 --> 00:31:54.310
impossible, or out
there beyond you.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:55.745
Did you want to
add anything else?

00:31:55.745 --> 00:31:57.370
