00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:22.370
MELANIE WARRICK: Mark,
question of the week?

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:23.325
MARK MANDEL:
Question of the week.

00:34:23.325 --> 00:34:25.449
MELANIE WARRICK: If I
want to create a GKE

00:34:25.449 --> 00:34:28.389
cluster with a specific
major Kubernetes version--

00:34:28.389 --> 00:34:29.920
or even just the latest--

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:31.929
using the command line
tools, how do I do that?

00:34:31.929 --> 00:34:33.580
MARK MANDEL: This is
something that I've run into,

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:34.785
and I was exploring it.

00:34:34.785 --> 00:34:35.320
MELANIE WARRICK: Really?

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:36.429
Did you run into this?

00:34:36.429 --> 00:34:37.060
MARK MANDEL: I had no idea.

00:34:37.060 --> 00:34:38.018
I really ran into this.

00:34:38.018 --> 00:34:39.469
[CAR CRASH NOISE]

00:34:39.469 --> 00:34:39.969
Right.

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:43.929
So GKE runs very specific
versions of Kubernetes.

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:47.420
So for example, 1.10
is available right now.

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:50.761
So if I'm running on the GKE,
I might be running 1.10.6.

00:34:50.761 --> 00:34:52.510
And then usually,
there's a patch release.

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:55.650
So like -gke.6, or .7, or .4.

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:58.660
And those can roll
through pretty quickly.

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:01.510
You may be like, on one
day, two or three weeks ago,

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:05.230
you're running 1.10.6-gke.1.

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:08.050
And then if you come back a few
weeks later, or a month later,

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:10.629
it might be gke.4,
and the old version

00:35:10.629 --> 00:35:11.920
might not be available anymore.

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:14.920
And you might have run scripts
either through the command

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:16.670
line-- or possibly
even Deployment Manager

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:18.120
as well-- that
are very specific.

00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:23.556
They might be like, OK, yes, I
need this version 1.10.7.gke-3.

00:35:23.556 --> 00:35:24.930
And that's not
available anymore,

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:27.263
and now, suddenly, all your
scripts break, and it sucks.

00:35:27.263 --> 00:35:28.210
[DRAMATIC MUSIC]

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:29.270
And I've run into
that as well, where

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:30.830
I have to update the
script every time I want

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:33.455
to create a cluster, either with
GCloud, or Deployment Manager.

00:35:33.455 --> 00:35:35.330
But you don't have to,
which is super nice.

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:35.870
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:35:35.870 --> 00:35:36.475
MARK MANDEL: It's amazing.

00:35:36.475 --> 00:35:37.400
MELANIE WARRICK: It's amazing.

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:38.280
[CELEBRATORY MUSIC]

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:39.821
MARK MANDEL: So the
really nice thing

00:35:39.821 --> 00:35:42.110
is you can actually specify
a general cluster version.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:45.800
So say, for example, I want
to create a version just like

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:46.430
a 1.10.

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:49.280
I'm like, I don't care which
like dot version of 1.10.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:50.330
Just create me a 1.10.

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:53.090
I can actually say, in my
initial cluster version

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:56.240
in Deployment Manager, or
cluster dash version in GCloud,

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.130
basically I can just
say, equals 1.10.

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:00.020
And it's just going to
grab the latest 1.10.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:03.380
If I want a patch
release, I can say 1.10.7,

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:05.440
and it'll always grab
the latest one of that,

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:07.580
and with the -gke on the end.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:10.090
Or if I just want the latest,
I can just write "latest."

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:10.310
MELANIE WARRICK: Hey.

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:10.760
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:12.970
So it'll just work it out
for you behind the scenes.

00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:14.420
It can save you a lot of
time, make your scripts

00:36:14.420 --> 00:36:16.400
a little less brittle, and
make things a little bit easier

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:16.640
for you.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:17.600
MELANIE WARRICK: We like easy.

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:17.840
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

